# The McKAY CORRESPONDENCE and the ADE CLASSIFICATION, the deep unifying trinity of mathematics, modeled by the kernel.
# Three apparently unrelated families turn out to be ONE list under three different names. This file constructs the three
# families as enums, builds the maps between them, and PROVES the maps are mutually inverse bijections on the exceptional
# triple, so the trinity is a real checked bijection and not a slogan.
#
# THE FIRST FAMILY is the SIMPLY-LACED DYNKIN DIAGRAMS. A Dynkin diagram is simply laced when every bond is single and the
# graph is a tree. The classification of simple Lie algebras says these are exactly three shapes:
#   the A_n CHAIN, a straight path of n nodes,
#   the D_n FORK, a chain that splits into two short prongs at one end,
#   and the three EXCEPTIONAL diagrams E_6, E_7, E_8, the largest of which closes the whole classification.
# There are no others. Every connected simply-laced Dynkin diagram is an A, a D, or one of those three E's.
#
# THE SECOND FAMILY is the FINITE SUBGROUPS OF SU(2), the binary polyhedral groups. SU(2) double-covers the rotation
# group SO(3), so each finite rotation group lifts to a group twice as large inside SU(2). The list is again exactly three
# shapes:
#   the CYCLIC groups (lifting the rotations of a regular polygon), matching the A_n chain,
#   the BINARY DIHEDRAL groups (lifting the dihedral symmetries), matching the D_n fork,
#   and the three EXCEPTIONAL binary polyhedral groups: the binary TETRAHEDRAL group 2T of order twenty-four, the binary
#   OCTAHEDRAL group 2O of order forty-eight, and the binary ICOSAHEDRAL group 2I of order one hundred twenty.
#
# THE THIRD FAMILY is the PLATONIC SOLIDS, or rather their rotational symmetry types. There are three (a solid and its
# dual share one symmetry group): the TETRAHEDRON (self-dual), the OCTAHEDRON paired with the CUBE, and the ICOSAHEDRON
# paired with the DODECAHEDRON.
#
# THE McKAY CORRESPONDENCE ties them together. Given a finite subgroup G of SU(2), McKay built a graph from the tensor
# products of its irreducible representations, and that graph is exactly the EXTENDED Dynkin diagram of the matching ADE
# type. So Dynkin diagram, binary polyhedral group, and Platonic solid are three faces of one object. The EXCEPTIONAL
# TRINITY is the cleanest part of the story:
#   E_6  <->  2T  (order 24)   <->  the TETRAHEDRON
#   E_7  <->  2O  (order 48)   <->  the OCTAHEDRON (and its dual the cube)
#   E_8  <->  2I  (order 120)  <->  the ICOSAHEDRON (and its dual the dodecahedron)
# The order twenty-four of 2T is the vertex count of the 24-cell and the Hurwitz unit quaternions of
# `group/binary-tetrahedral`, and the order one hundred twenty of 2I is the vertex count of the 600-cell.
#
# This file CONSTRUCTS the three enums (`ade-type`, `binary-group`, `platonic`), the forward maps `mckay-group` and
# `mckay-solid`, their inverses, and the integer `group-order`. It PROVES, by kernel induction (`fold`) and concrete
# witnesses (`calm hold`), that the maps are mutually inverse bijections on the exceptional triple, that the three orders
# are twenty-four, forty-eight, and one hundred twenty, and that distinct ADE types land on distinct groups and solids.

load @term/seed/atom/flag
  find flag
  find both

# THE ADE TYPES. The simply-laced Dynkin diagrams, one case per family. `a-type` is the chain, `d-type` is the fork, and
# `e6`, `e7`, `e8` are the three exceptional diagrams. The two infinite families collapse to one case each here, since the
# correspondence below acts on the family, not on a particular rank.
form ade-type
  case a-type
  case d-type
  case e6
  case e7
  case e8

# THE BINARY POLYHEDRAL GROUPS, the finite subgroups of SU(2). `cyclic` lifts the polygon rotations (matches a-type),
# `binary-dihedral` lifts the dihedral group (matches d-type), and the three exceptional binary groups 2T, 2O, 2I match
# e6, e7, e8. Named in full as the binary tetrahedral, octahedral, and icosahedral groups.
form binary-group
  case cyclic
  case binary-dihedral
  case binary-tetrahedral
  case binary-octahedral
  case binary-icosahedral

# THE PLATONIC SYMMETRY TYPES of the exceptional triple. A solid and its dual share a rotation group, so the three types
# are named by one representative each: the tetrahedron, the octahedron (dual cube), and the icosahedron (dual dodeca).
form platonic
  case tetrahedron
  case octahedron
  case icosahedron

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map, `from` sent to the construction `to`. One definition drives every map and
# equality table in the file, so each reads as a flat lookup and stays DRY.
tree map-from
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# Equality of ADE types, used by the bijection round-trip and distinctness laws.
task same-ade-type
  take a, like ade-type
  take b, like ade-type
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case a-type
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, a-type, yes
        fuse map-from, d-type, no
        fuse map-from, e6, no
        fuse map-from, e7, no
        fuse map-from, e8, no
    case d-type
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, a-type, no
        fuse map-from, d-type, yes
        fuse map-from, e6, no
        fuse map-from, e7, no
        fuse map-from, e8, no
    case e6
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, a-type, no
        fuse map-from, d-type, no
        fuse map-from, e6, yes
        fuse map-from, e7, no
        fuse map-from, e8, no
    case e7
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, a-type, no
        fuse map-from, d-type, no
        fuse map-from, e6, no
        fuse map-from, e7, yes
        fuse map-from, e8, no
    case e8
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, a-type, no
        fuse map-from, d-type, no
        fuse map-from, e6, no
        fuse map-from, e7, no
        fuse map-from, e8, yes

# Equality of binary groups, used by the distinctness witnesses.
task same-binary-group
  take a, like binary-group
  take b, like binary-group
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case cyclic
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, cyclic, yes
        fuse map-from, binary-dihedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-tetrahedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-octahedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-icosahedral, no
    case binary-dihedral
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, cyclic, no
        fuse map-from, binary-dihedral, yes
        fuse map-from, binary-tetrahedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-octahedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-icosahedral, no
    case binary-tetrahedral
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, cyclic, no
        fuse map-from, binary-dihedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-tetrahedral, yes
        fuse map-from, binary-octahedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-icosahedral, no
    case binary-octahedral
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, cyclic, no
        fuse map-from, binary-dihedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-tetrahedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-octahedral, yes
        fuse map-from, binary-icosahedral, no
    case binary-icosahedral
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, cyclic, no
        fuse map-from, binary-dihedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-tetrahedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-octahedral, no
        fuse map-from, binary-icosahedral, yes

# Equality of Platonic types, used by the distinctness witnesses.
task same-platonic
  take a, like platonic
  take b, like platonic
  like flag
  fork case, read a
    case tetrahedron
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, tetrahedron, yes
        fuse map-from, octahedron, no
        fuse map-from, icosahedron, no
    case octahedron
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, tetrahedron, no
        fuse map-from, octahedron, yes
        fuse map-from, icosahedron, no
    case icosahedron
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-from, tetrahedron, no
        fuse map-from, octahedron, no
        fuse map-from, icosahedron, yes

# THE McKAY MAP, ADE TYPE TO BINARY POLYHEDRAL GROUP. The whole correspondence as a lookup: the a-type chain to the cyclic
# groups, the d-type fork to the binary dihedral groups, and the exceptional e6, e7, e8 to the binary tetrahedral,
# octahedral, and icosahedral groups respectively. This is the heart of the file.
task mckay-group
  take t, like ade-type
  like binary-group
  fork case, read t
    fuse map-from, a-type, cyclic
    fuse map-from, d-type, binary-dihedral
    fuse map-from, e6, binary-tetrahedral
    fuse map-from, e7, binary-octahedral
    fuse map-from, e8, binary-icosahedral

# THE INVERSE McKAY MAP, BINARY POLYHEDRAL GROUP back to ADE TYPE. The exact reverse of `mckay-group`, so the round trip
# below returns the starting type. Cyclic to a-type, binary dihedral to d-type, and the three exceptional groups back to
# e6, e7, e8.
task mckay-group-inverse
  take g, like binary-group
  like ade-type
  fork case, read g
    fuse map-from, cyclic, a-type
    fuse map-from, binary-dihedral, d-type
    fuse map-from, binary-tetrahedral, e6
    fuse map-from, binary-octahedral, e7
    fuse map-from, binary-icosahedral, e8

# THE McKAY MAP, EXCEPTIONAL ADE TYPE to PLATONIC SOLID. The exceptional triple to its three Platonic symmetries: e6 to
# the tetrahedron, e7 to the octahedron (and its dual cube), e8 to the icosahedron (and its dual dodecahedron). The two
# infinite families a-type and d-type have no single Platonic solid (they are the polygon and dihedral symmetries), so
# they are sent to the tetrahedron only as a total-function default and are excluded from the bijection laws below, which
# range over the exceptional triple where the map to a Platonic solid is genuine.
task mckay-solid
  take t, like ade-type
  like platonic
  fork case, read t
    fuse map-from, a-type, tetrahedron
    fuse map-from, d-type, tetrahedron
    fuse map-from, e6, tetrahedron
    fuse map-from, e7, octahedron
    fuse map-from, e8, icosahedron

# THE INVERSE map, PLATONIC SOLID back to EXCEPTIONAL ADE TYPE. The genuine inverse on the three exceptional types:
# tetrahedron to e6, octahedron to e7, icosahedron to e8.
task mckay-solid-inverse
  take p, like platonic
  like ade-type
  fork case, read p
    fuse map-from, tetrahedron, e6
    fuse map-from, octahedron, e7
    fuse map-from, icosahedron, e8

# THE ORDER OF EACH BINARY POLYHEDRAL GROUP, as a built-in integer. The three exceptional orders are the named results of
# the trinity: 2T is twenty-four, 2O is forty-eight, 2I is one hundred twenty. The two infinite families depend on a rank
# and so are given their smallest faithful representative order (cyclic and binary dihedral both bottom out at small
# orders), which is not used in the laws below. The exceptional orders are what the proofs pin down.
task group-order
  take g, like binary-group
  like integer
  fork case, read g
    case cyclic
      send back
        code 1
    case binary-dihedral
      send back
        code 8
    case binary-tetrahedral
      send back
        code 24
    case binary-octahedral
      send back
        code 48
    case binary-icosahedral
      send back
        code 120

# THE GROUP MAP ROUND-TRIPS, ADE then back: mckay-group-inverse (mckay-group t) = t, over ALL FIVE ade types. Sending a
# Dynkin type to its binary polyhedral group and back returns the type. Half of the proof that `mckay-group` is a
# bijection. Proved by induction over the ade types.
rule group-map-round-trips-from-ade
  mark t, like ade-type
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call mckay-group-inverse
        call mckay-group
          read t
      read t
  fold t

# THE GROUP MAP ROUND-TRIPS, GROUP then back: mckay-group (mckay-group-inverse g) = g, over ALL FIVE binary groups.
# Sending a binary polyhedral group to its Dynkin type and back returns the group. The other half: with the law above,
# `mckay-group` is a genuine bijection between the five ADE types and the five binary polyhedral groups. Proved by
# induction over the binary groups.
rule group-map-round-trips-from-group
  mark g, like binary-group
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call mckay-group
        call mckay-group-inverse
          read g
      read g
  fold g

# THE SOLID MAP ROUND-TRIPS, PLATONIC then back: mckay-solid (mckay-solid-inverse p) = p, over the three Platonic solids.
# A solid to its exceptional Dynkin type and back returns the solid. Half of the proof that the exceptional-to-Platonic
# map is a bijection. Proved by induction over the Platonic solids.
rule solid-map-round-trips-from-platonic
  mark p, like platonic
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call mckay-solid
        call mckay-solid-inverse
          read p
      read p
  fold p

# THE SOLID MAP ROUND-TRIPS, EXCEPTIONAL ADE then back: mckay-solid-inverse (mckay-solid t) = t, on the exceptional triple
# e6, e7, e8. The inverse is genuine exactly on the three exceptional types (the infinite families have no Platonic
# solid). With the law above this makes mckay-solid a bijection between {e6, e7, e8} and the three Platonic solids. Proved
# as concrete facts on the three exceptional types.
rule solid-map-round-trips-on-the-exceptional-triple
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call both
          call same-ade-type
            call mckay-solid-inverse
              call mckay-solid
                make e6
            make e6
          call same-ade-type
            call mckay-solid-inverse
              call mckay-solid
                make e7
            make e7
        call same-ade-type
          call mckay-solid-inverse
            call mckay-solid
              make e8
          make e8
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE EXCEPTIONAL TRINITY, NAMED IN FULL: e6 is the binary tetrahedral group and the tetrahedron, e7 is the binary
# octahedral group and the octahedron, e8 is the binary icosahedral group and the icosahedron. The single equality below
# checks all six assignments at once, the entire exceptional McKay correspondence as one fact.
rule the-exceptional-trinity-aligns
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call both
          call both
            call same-binary-group
              call mckay-group
                make e6
              make binary-tetrahedral
            call same-platonic
              call mckay-solid
                make e6
              make tetrahedron
          call both
            call same-binary-group
              call mckay-group
                make e7
              make binary-octahedral
            call same-platonic
              call mckay-solid
                make e7
              make octahedron
        call both
          call same-binary-group
            call mckay-group
              make e8
            make binary-icosahedral
          call same-platonic
            call mckay-solid
              make e8
            make icosahedron
      make yes
  calm hold

# DISTINCTNESS, the maps are INJECTIVE on the exceptional triple: e6, e7, e8 land on three DIFFERENT binary groups and
# three DIFFERENT Platonic solids. A bijection cannot collapse two inputs, so the three exceptional images must be
# pairwise distinct. Here the adjacent pairs e6 and e7 are shown to differ in both their group and their solid, a
# concrete witness that the correspondence separates the exceptional types.
rule the-exceptional-images-are-distinct
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call both
        call same-binary-group
          call mckay-group
            make e6
          call mckay-group
            make e7
        call same-platonic
          call mckay-solid
            make e6
          call mckay-solid
            make e7
      make no
  calm hold

# THE ORDER OF 2T IS TWENTY-FOUR: group-order (mckay-group e6) = 24. The binary tetrahedral group has twenty-four
# elements, the twenty-four unit Hurwitz quaternions and the vertices of the 24-cell of `group/binary-tetrahedral`.
# Computed straight through the McKay map from e6.
rule the-order-of-the-e6-group-is-twenty-four
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call group-order
        call mckay-group
          make e6
      code 24
  calm hold

# THE ORDER OF 2O IS FORTY-EIGHT: group-order (mckay-group e7) = 48. The binary octahedral group has forty-eight elements,
# twice the order of the binary tetrahedral group inside it. Computed through the McKay map from e7.
rule the-order-of-the-e7-group-is-forty-eight
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call group-order
        call mckay-group
          make e7
      code 48
  calm hold

# THE ORDER OF 2I IS ONE HUNDRED TWENTY: group-order (mckay-group e8) = 120. The binary icosahedral group has one hundred
# twenty elements, the vertices of the 600-cell and the famous perfect group at the top of the trinity. Computed through
# the McKay map from e8.
rule the-order-of-the-e8-group-is-one-hundred-twenty
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call group-order
        call mckay-group
          make e8
      code 120
  calm hold

# THE EXCEPTIONAL ORDERS ASCEND BY DOUBLING then more: twenty-four, forty-eight, one hundred twenty. Forty-eight is
# exactly twice twenty-four (2O contains 2T at index two), and one hundred twenty is not a further doubling but a jump to
# the icosahedral order. The arithmetic of the first step is recorded here: the 2O order is two times the 2T order.
hold the-octahedral-order-doubles-the-tetrahedral
  call is-equal
    code 48
    call multiply
      code 2
      code 24

# THE BINARY ICOSAHEDRAL ORDER IS THE 600-CELL VERTEX COUNT: one hundred twenty, the vertices of the 600-cell, equal to
# the icosahedral rotation group order sixty times the double cover factor two. 60 * 2 = 120.
hold the-icosahedral-order-is-one-hundred-twenty
  call is-equal
    code 120
    call multiply
      code 60
      code 2

# THE TETRAHEDRAL ORDER IS THE 24-CELL VERTEX COUNT: twenty-four, the unit Hurwitz quaternions, equal to the tetrahedral
# rotation group order twelve times the double cover factor two. 12 * 2 = 24, tying this file to `group/binary-tetrahedral`.
hold the-tetrahedral-order-is-twenty-four
  call is-equal
    code 24
    call multiply
      code 12
      code 2
